{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The outage that got worse the moment it started</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Your app keeps a live WebSocket connection open, and reconnects the instant it drops, seems reasonable, you want to be reconnected as fast as possible. Then the server has a brief hiccup and drops every client's connection at once. Every single client reconnects immediately, all at the same moment, and that flood of simultaneous reconnect attempts is often what turns a brief hiccup into a real outage.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Immediate reconnect on every disconnect works fine for the common case, a single client's connection dropping for its own reasons. It fails badly for the correlated case, a server-side event that disconnects many clients simultaneously, since "many clients reconnecting instantly" is functionally a self-inflicted traffic spike at the worst possible moment.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      A recovering server needs headroom to actually recover. An instant-retry client population removes exactly that headroom, every reconnect attempt competes with the server's own recovery work for the same limited resources.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Back off exponentially, and spread attempts out with jitter</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of reconnecting instantly every time, wait a little longer after each failed attempt, 1 second, then 2, then 4, then 8, and so on. This gives a struggling server progressively more breathing room instead of a constant hammering.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Exponential backoff alone still has a problem: every client computes the exact same delay sequence, so they all retry in sync, still a synchronized spike, just a less frequent one. Adding jitter, a small random offset to each computed delay, spreads the retries out across time instead of them landing in the same instant.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      "Full jitter" (a random delay uniformly chosen between 0 and the exponential cap, rather than the exponential value plus a small random wobble) tends to outperform smaller-jitter variants under real correlated-failure load, because it spreads the retry distribution much wider rather than clustering near the exponential curve's exact value.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The five-second outage that took twenty minutes to recover from</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's realtime dashboard used a WebSocket client that reconnected immediately on any disconnect, no delay at all. A routine server restart dropped every connected client for about five seconds. When the server came back up, it was immediately hit by the full reconnect flood, thousands of clients reconnecting in the same second, and the resulting load spike itself caused the server to become unresponsive again.
    </p>
    <p>
      This repeated in a loop, brief recovery, immediate flood, unresponsive again, for roughly twenty minutes before the team manually throttled connections at the load balancer to break the cycle.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The actual fix was exponential backoff with full jitter on the client side, capped at a reasonable maximum delay (30 seconds), so a real five-second server restart now resolves in roughly that same five seconds, clients spread their reconnect attempts out instead of arriving as one synchronized wave.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They also added a reconnect counter reset on any successfully-sustained connection (staying connected for more than some threshold resets the backoff to its minimum), so a client that reconnects successfully after a brief drop doesn't stay artificially throttled the next time it needs to reconnect for an unrelated reason.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

