{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Backoff needs a ceiling, and a reset condition</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Always cap the maximum delay (30-60 seconds is common). Uncapped exponential backoff eventually makes a client wait absurdly long between attempts, which feels broken from the user's side even though it's technically "working as designed."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Reset the backoff counter once a connection has stayed up for a meaningful duration, otherwise a client that hits one bad patch stays throttled on every subsequent, unrelated disconnect for the rest of the session.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Distinguish reconnect reasons where possible, a clean server-initiated close code (like a deploy) can reasonably skip the initial backoff step entirely on the assumption the server is intentionally cycling, while an abnormal close or repeated failures should always back off, conflating every disconnect reason into one retry policy under- or over-reacts depending on the actual cause.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

